== Apparatus
<apparatus>
Auditory and tactile stimuli were delivered through a Komplete Audio 6 MK2 USB audio interface @komplete_audio_6_mk2. The audio channel was routed to a pair of Sennheiser HD 560S open-back reference headphones @sennheiser_hd560s, worn over the ears, and the tactile channel was routed to a Woojer Strap 4 haptic transducer @woojer_strap4, worn over the chest just above the breast area. Placement was standardised across participants by following the positioning illustration printed on the strap itself, which the operator used as a fitting guide. Participant responses were collected with a standard USB mouse. Respiratory compliance was monitored throughout the session with a chest-mounted respiratory belt transducer positioned around the lower ribcage. In the Viscereality condition, participants additionally wore a head-mounted display delivering the breath-coupled visual environment. In the Control condition, participants viewed the same dark field used as the black-screen control in Study~3, containing only the central breathing performance indicator that scaled with respiratory excursion. This provided minimal feedback stimuli for breath performance without the spatial depth cues, color, or aesthetic properties of the Viscereality environment, and participants did not wear an eye shade (Fig.~#ref(<study5:fig:exp_setup>, supplement: none)).

== Stimulus generation
<study5:sec:stim_gen>
The box-breathing guide was built from two pre-recorded human-voice audio files, one for the inhalation half-cycle and one for the exhalation half-cycle @pydub_audiosegment_note. Each file voiced a count of "two, three, four, hold" over four seconds so that a full 4--4--4--4 cycle of inhalation, post-inhalation hold, exhalation, and post-exhalation hold lasted sixteen seconds. Files were loaded at their source rate and resampled to 44.1~kHz using `librosa` where available and otherwise `scipy.signal.resample` @Virtanen2020SciPy@librosa_resample_note. Amplitude was attenuated to 30% of source level so that the voice guide remained audible without masking the looming stimuli. The guide occupied the right channel of the per-trial stereo WAV, with the left channel set to silence over the same four-second span.

Binaural looming stimuli were initially synthesised from four noise types, namely pink, white, brown, and blue noise; later acquisition versions used a subset, predominantly pink and white @brinkmann2017fabian. For each noise type, a 3-second trajectory was rendered in which a point source approached the head frontally from 1.1~m to 0.1~m at a constant 33.3~cm/s, at zero azimuth and zero elevation. Source-to-ear distances were computed for an 18~cm interaural distance, and amplitude was scaled by the inverse square of source-to-ear distance. Head-related transfer function filtering used the measured FABIAN head-related impulse response at neutral head-above-torso orientation, specifically the `FABIAN_HRIR_measured_HATO_0.sofa` file from the FABIAN database hosted on the Technische Universität Berlin DepositOnce repository @fabian_database_2017@brinkmann2017fabian. The SOFA file was parsed with the `sofar` Python library @sofar_python, the left and right impulse responses for the azimuth-zero, elevation-zero position were extracted, and each was convolved with the per-channel noise trajectory using `scipy.signal.fftconvolve` @Virtanen2020SciPy. Each looming file was normalised to 95% of maximum amplitude to prevent digital clipping and was padded with 500~ms of silence before and after the 3-second approach, yielding a 4-second stimulus segment.

Tactile pulses were generated for the Woojer Strap 4 transducer, whose resonance is near 50~Hz. Each pulse was a 100~ms pure-tone 50~Hz sine at digital amplitude 1.0 with an instant-on, instant-off block envelope, chosen to deliver the maximum mechanical impulse the transducer supports @woojer_strap4. The stimulus-generation scripts can instantiate pulses at 0, 300, 800, 1500, 2200, and 2700~ms after looming onset, with the 0~ms file serving as an audio--tactile simultaneity stimulus. The participant master blocks used the five non-zero PPS SOAs, namely 300, 800, 1500, 2200, and 2700~ms, so that tactile cues sampled the approaching sound after it had begun moving toward the body.

Each trial was compiled as an 8-second stereo WAV file at 44.1~kHz in 32-bit float using `soundfile` @sounddevice_python. The file contained a 4-second breathing-guide segment followed by a 4-second stimulus segment. Within the stimulus segment, the looming noise occupied the right channel and the tactile pulse occupied the left channel, positioned at the trial-specific SOA within the 4-second window. Baseline trials carried the tactile pulse without a looming sound, and catch trials carried the looming sound without a tactile pulse. Total trial duration was verified at assembly to within one millisecond of the 352,800-sample target.

Source-timing inspection revealed a qualification to the nominal cue schedule. In 167 hash-verified original block recordings from 14 cleared participants, tactile pulses were matched to the archived played-order CSVs; all block-median Audio--Tactile tactile cues occurred 500~ms later within the eight-second trial than Baseline cues with the same nominal SOA label. An independently decoded session from a fifteenth cleared participant showed the same block-median offset in all 12 comparable blocks. The archived generator loads looming inputs already padded by 500~ms and has a path that prepends another 500~ms to assembled Audio--Tactile trials while Baseline trials receive one prepend. This shifts absolute cue time while preserving the within-trial auditory-to-tactile SOA, verified directly where both onsets were decoded in the independent session. The other 25 cleared participants lack comparable cue-time verification, so shared nominal labels cannot establish matched absolute timing across trial types in the full cohort.

Per-participant trial sequences were generated under a three-level randomisation logic intended to preserve the planned design at the session level while limiting trial-to-trial predictability within a block. At the macro level, the Condition $times$ respiratory Phase $times$ five-SOA grid for Audio--Tactile trials and the trial-type counts were fixed across full sessions. Available noise types varied by acquisition version, so sound-type balance was version-specific rather than common to every participant. Within each block, trial order was drawn from a Gellermann-inspired constrained randomisation in which post-inhalation and post-exhalation trials were interleaved, with the algorithm rejecting any sequence that placed more than three consecutive trials of the same type or distributed a trial type asymmetrically across the two halves of a block @gellermann_1933. Each session was organised as two parts of six blocks each (12 blocks in total), with the six blocks within each part labelled a through f, and each participant was assigned one block-order permutation drawn from the 720 orderings of the six block labels, applied within both parts. This block-order randomisation distributes any block-level position effects across participants without disturbing the within-block constraints. Each block was concatenated into a single continuous WAV of the stimulus-delivery length, accompanied by a trial-order CSV that recorded the trial type, SOA, noise type, and respiratory phase per trial.

A residual source of predictability is unavoidable in this design. The 4--4--4--4 box-breathing rhythm fixes the within-cycle position of the breath-hold phases at which stimuli can be delivered, so participants can in principle anticipate #emph[when];, within each respiratory cycle, an audio--tactile event might arrive. Removing this regularity is not feasible, because a stable respiratory cycle is the precondition for systematically assessing inhale versus exhale as a test variable; an irregular cycle would compromise phase assignment, which is the central manipulation of the study. We therefore adopted the regular 4--4--4--4 rhythm and limited predictability by other means. #emph[Which] stimulus arrives was randomised across the noise types available in each acquisition version. #emph[Whether] a tactile pulse arrives at all was randomised through the inclusion of catch trials (sound-only, no vibration), which required response inhibition. Together with the within-block constrained randomisation and the per-participant block-order permutation, these manipulations limited cues that a participant could exploit to anticipate any individual trial.

== Trial design
<trial-design>
The experiment used a within-subjects design adapted from the protocol used to test PPS modulation by vestibular stimulation @pfeiffer2018vestibular, modified for scheduled audio--tactile measurement during box breathing. Each full session planned 408 trials, organised into two parts of six blocks each (12 blocks of 34 trials), with 240 Audio--Tactile trials, 120 Baseline trials, and 48 Catch trials. Audio--Tactile trials delivered a looming sound followed by a tactile pulse at one of five SOAs (300, 800, 1500, 2200, and 2700~ms; #box[#cite(<lee2021altered>, form: "author")] #box[#cite(<lee2021altered>, form: "year")]#text[;] #box[#cite(<mine2022adaptation>, form: "author")] #box[#cite(<mine2022adaptation>, form: "year")]#text[;] #box[#cite(<teneggi2013social>, form: "author")] #box[#cite(<teneggi2013social>, form: "year")]). The initial acquisition version used blue, brown, pink, and white spectral noise; later versions predominantly used pink and white, the two sound types present across the whole publication cohort. Within each version, Audio--Tactile trials were planned across the 2 (respiratory phase) $times$ 5 (SOA) design grid at 24 trials per phase $times$ SOA cell. Baseline trials delivered only the tactile pulse, used the same five nominal delay labels, and were balanced across respiratory phase (60 inhale, 60 exhale). They provide a tactile-only response reference but have no auditory distance; their absolute cue timing cannot be presumed equal to Audio--Tactile trials at the same label. Catch trials delivered only the looming sound and required response inhibition (48 planned trials), yielding a planned catch rate of approximately 12%. Each trial lasted 8 seconds, so a full 408-trial session took approximately 54 minutes of stimulus time excluding block-level breaks and instructions (Fig.~#ref(<study5:fig:trial_design>, supplement: none)). One included session ended before all 408 planned trials had been observed; its missing slots and an exclusion sensitivity are documented in the Results.

