== Stimulus delivery and response capture
<stimulus-delivery-and-response-capture>
At run time, each per-block WAV was fully pre-loaded into memory and streamed to the Komplete Audio 6 MK2 on WASAPI via the `sounddevice` Python binding to the PortAudio library @sounddevice_python, with a 4096-sample callback block size and a target output latency of 100~ms for stable long-block playback. Python swapped the two channels at the callback boundary so that the left channel of the file was sent to physical Output 2, wired to the Woojer Strap 4 haptic transducer, and the right channel of the file was sent to physical Output 1, wired to the Sennheiser HD 560S headphones. The audio and tactile streams originated from the same file and passed through the same stereo callback, preserving their sample alignment in the digital output stream. Relative acoustic and mechanical onset latencies at the participant were not independently measured.

Mouse clicks were monitored with the `pynput` library @pynput_python, and each click triggered a 1200~Hz, 50~ms synthetic click tone on a separate low-latency `sounddevice` stream that was routed only to Output 2. The click tone served two purposes. It provided the participant with immediate auditory feedback that the response had registered, and it inserted a detectable event onto the tactile channel that was captured in the loopback recording described below and used for post-hoc reaction-time estimation.

Before participant playback, the selected stereo output profile was required to pass a routing preflight in which calibration pulses were sent separately to Output~1 and Output~2 and verified through the corresponding WASAPI loopback endpoint. The documented acquisition profile used the Komplete Audio 6 MK2, with Output~1 assigned to the headphones and Output~2 assigned to the tactile transducer, but the same software could be run on another wired two-channel interface only after the same channel-separation preflight passed. Once participant playback began, the acquisition software was designed to mirror both physical outputs to a WASAPI loopback device and capture them through the `PyAudioWPatch` extension of the PortAudio interface @pyaudiowpatch. These source recordings are not presently linked and independently checked for every dataset in the publication pool. Available session WAV files and behavioural CSVs were written to participant-specific data folders with identifying metadata; only coded participant IDs appear in analysis outputs.

== Reaction-time reconstruction from loopback recordings
<reaction-time-reconstruction-from-loopback-recordings>
For the loopback data to which the waveform-decoding pipeline was applied, reaction times were reconstructed in Python using `numpy` and `scipy` @Virtanen2020SciPy. In that pipeline, audio and tactile channels were identified by the ratio of mean absolute amplitude to root-mean-square amplitude, with the sparser channel assigned as tactile. Tactile cue events were then detected by smoothing the absolute tactile signal with a 10~ms rectangular envelope and thresholding at 0.45 of the envelope peak, subject to a minimum run of 60~ms and a minimum peak amplitude. The resulting cue-onset train was fitted to an 8-second trial grid by jointly minimising the median absolute error between detected onsets and the nominal SOA set, yielding a per-trial cue-onset time in the sample domain of the recorded WAV.

For each trial processed by that pipeline, the candidate looming templates for its acquisition version were trimmed to their first 1.5~s of non-silent content, downsampled fivefold for speed, and z-standardised. Each template was cross-correlated with the audio channel of the recording inside the 4.45~s to 7.85~s window relative to the trial origin, using frequency-domain convolution @Virtanen2020SciPy. The highest-scoring template above threshold assigned the trial its observed noise type and returned the template-onset timestamp, giving an independent anchor for SOA verification where such recording evidence was available.

Where the loopback recording and both event markers were available, the 1200~Hz, 50~ms click tone template was z-standardised and cross-correlated with the tactile channel starting at each detected cue onset. Click events were accepted when the local correlation peak exceeded a dynamic threshold of eight median absolute deviations above the local median and were separated from any prior click by at least 100~ms. For such recordings, reaction time was the sample-domain difference between click-tone onset and tactile-cue onset, converted to milliseconds at 44,100~Hz. The pooled publication dataset also contains source-recorded reaction times from acquisition versions for which this exact waveform-based definition has not been independently re-harmonised. Consequently the sample-domain timing advantage cannot be assumed for every pooled trial; source-specific timing definitions and verification status are retained in the private provenance crosswalk.

An earlier 40-person source audit catalogued original-recording origins for 31 participants and verified at least one asset hash for each of 17 early-version sessions. That historical audit documented RT-onset variants and unavailable original assets; it is not a new audit of the released 42-person cohort. Source dates, waveform verification status, and acquisition-version identities are deliberately absent from the public tables. The browser companion therefore reports the limits of source-recorded RTs without reconstructing private provenance from arbitrary participant IDs. A file hash establishes source identity, not waveform remeasurement or respiratory-phase accuracy.

Trials were labelled against their scheduled trial order and, where available, checked against runtime and recording evidence. Audio--Tactile and Baseline trials with a detected response were labelled hits; Catch trials with a response were false alarms. Unobserved planned slots were kept explicit in the participant audit tables and excluded from reaction-time analysis. Hit-labelled trials without a recorded reaction time contributed to response-quality summaries but not to reaction-time models; missing reaction times were not imputed. The publication-pool analysis table contains only observed planned trials, with trial type, SOA, noise type, scheduled respiratory phase, condition, response outcome, and source-recorded reaction time. Event-marker availability and source location are recorded separately; the analysis table does not imply that every reaction time was newly decoded from a common waveform.

== Peripersonal-space quantification
<peripersonal-space-quantification>
Peripersonal space was operationalised as SOA-dependent audio--tactile reaction time in a looming-sound tactile-detection paradigm @canzoneri2012dynamic@teneggi2013social@noel2015peripersonal. The tactile pulse occurred at one of five nominal SOAs (300, 800, 1500, 2200, or 2700~ms); longer SOAs corresponded to nearer nominal source positions. The registration labels these SOAs as approximately 100, 73, 50, 27, and 10~cm, although its stated linear 110-to-10~cm, three-second trajectory instead implies approximately 100, 83, 60, 37, and 20~cm at those times. The primary predictor was therefore nominal SOA in milliseconds, not an inferred physical distance. The browser sigmoid implementation retains the registered distance labels for its viability gate; a sensitivity repeated that gate using trajectory-implied positions. It subtracts mean tactile-only Baseline reaction time within each participant $times$ Condition $times$ Phase cell: $upright(R T)_(upright(A T)) - upright(R T)_(upright(b a s e l i n e))$, with negative values indicating faster Audio--Tactile responses. Baseline trials have no auditory stimulus or acoustic distance. We also compared Audio--Tactile and Baseline RTs at the same nominal SOA label descriptively, without treating the labels as proof of equal cue time. Misses, Catch outcomes, and unobserved planned slots were retained for quality summaries but did not supply reaction times to these curves.

== Questionnaire scoring and exploratory analysis
<questionnaire-scoring-and-exploratory-analysis>
The released participant table contains one set of 37 MAIA-2 items and paired Control/Viscereality spatial-frame-of-reference ratings. The project owner confirmed that MAIA-2 was completed once before the experiment and spatial-frame ratings after each intervention; this timing is supplied by the owner rather than reconstructed from CSV timestamps. MAIA-2 assesses eight self-reported interoceptive dimensions @Mehling2012@Mehling2018; such reports should be distinguished from objective interoceptive accuracy and metacognitive awareness @Garfinkel2015. Scores on the administered 0--5 response scale were independently recomputed using the final MAIA-2 item map: Noticing, items 1--4; Not-Distracting, 5--10; Not-Worrying, 11--15; Attention Regulation, 16--22; Emotional Awareness, 23--27; Self-Regulation, 28--31; Body Listening, 32--34; and Trusting, 35--37. Items 5--10, 11, 12 and 15 were reversed as $5 - upright(r e s p o n s e)$. Each subscale required all constituent items; missing responses were not imputed and no total score was formed. Recomputed and stored means were compared within $10^(- 6)$. The release does not supply the administered language record, repeated MAIA scores, or separate body-boundary and small-self fields.

This questionnaire extension is post hoc. Its scoring, estimands and test family were recorded before computing the new associations, but after inspecting the dataset and prior behavioural results. The original registration endpoint returned HTTP 403 during this review; its exact questionnaire wording could not be independently checked. The current repository Methods and analysis contract supply the available account of the plan, with the distinction between registered behavioural questions and this exploratory extension retained. The original registration and administered instrument records require verification before submission.

We reported item frequencies, missingness, subscale descriptives, corrected item--rest correlations and raw Cronbach alpha. Sample-specific reliability can differ by dimension and occasion @Eggart2021; alpha also has restrictive assumptions @McNeish2018. We therefore reported pointwise percentile 95% intervals from 2,000 whole-participant bootstrap draws for means and alpha, without treating a cutoff as evidence of validity, deleting items, or fitting a new 37-item factor structure in this small sample. No clinical change thresholds or pre/post tests were transferred from external validation studies.

Spatial-frame ratings are relevant to experiential changes studied in mindfulness research @hanley_2020, but the present release includes only condition-labelled A--H responses and corresponding codes 1--8. We verified agreement of these encodings and retained their ordinal order. Category distributions and the full paired transition table were reported. A two-sided exact binomial sign test compared higher versus lower Viscereality ratings among non-ties, with a Clopper--Pearson interval for the higher-rating proportion. Ties remained in the overall paired denominator and in paired dominance, defined as $\( n_(upright(h i g h e r)) - n_(upright(l o w e r)) \) \/ n_(upright(p a i r s))$, with a participant-bootstrap interval. Order-stratified counts were descriptive. Equal spacing of categories was not assumed. Because the administered pictogram key is unavailable, higher codes were not labelled allocentric.

The retained supplementary association set comprised 33 Spearman correlations: eight MAIA-2 dimensions with each of Control rating, Viscereality rating, rating-change direction ($- 1 , 0 , + 1$), and the equal-participant raw-RT Condition $times$ Phase SOA-slope contrast; plus rating-change direction with that behavioural contrast. The slope contrast used the five cell means and primary SOA scaling; it does not estimate a sigmoid boundary. Complete pairs, average ranks, 19,999 pairing permutations, and the absolute-statistic two-sided tail with the plus-one correction supplied $p$ values. All 33 association $p$ values were reported without multiple-testing correction. This reporting choice replaced the initially specified Holm adjustment after inspecting the results; the analyses remain exploratory. The plus-one rule is part of Monte Carlo permutation inference, not adjustment across tests. Pointwise 95% intervals used 2,000 paired-person bootstrap draws with re-ranking, excluding and counting undefined draws. Legacy resampling used seed 20261116. These intervals are not simultaneous, and permutation inference assumes exchangeable participants under independence without covariate adjustment. The spatial condition comparison formed a separate, single exploratory test family. Inter-subscale correlations were descriptive without further testing. Missing questionnaires never excluded a person from the primary RT cohort; boundary-related correlations retained the original fit gate.

The main spatial follow-up was subsequently specified as the signed numerical difference $Delta_i = Y_(i , upright(V i s c e r e a l i t y)) - Y_(i , upright(C o n t r o l))$, regardless of presentation order. These are two post-intervention ratings, not change from a spatial baseline. This exploratory amendment was recorded before computing the new delta analyses but after inspecting the earlier questionnaire results. All eight continuous MAIA-2 dimensions were correlated separately with numerical $Delta$ using the same permutation and paired-bootstrap procedure, with an independent generator seeded 20261117. One ancillary correlation linked $Delta$ to the raw-RT SOA-slope interaction. The eight existing direction-only correlations were retained as ordinal sensitivities; the complete earlier set remained supplementary. Numerical subtraction assumes comparable category steps, and ranking the resulting differences does not eliminate that assumption.

Condition $times$ pre-experiment MAIA moderation was estimated separately for each dimension by OLS regression of participant $Delta$ on an intercept and mean-centred MAIA. A second specification added centred presentation order (0=Control-first, 1=Viscereality-first). Differencing eliminates participant intercepts; in this complete two-condition design, the MAIA slope tests the corresponding repeated-measures interaction, while the order coefficient represents Condition $times$ Order. Independent long-format participant-intercept fits verified the equivalence. Slopes are code steps per one MAIA point. Ordinary two-sided $t$ inference used $n - k$ residual degrees of freedom and interaction $F = t^2$ with $\( 1 , n - k \)$ degrees of freedom. This inference assumes a linear conditional mean, independent participants and homoskedastic Gaussian errors. HC3 covariance with $t_(n - k)$ tails supplied approximate heteroskedasticity-sensitive intervals and $p$ values. Both specifications and both uncertainty estimates were reported for every dimension, without multiple-testing correction. Maximum leverage and Cook distance were exported; no participant was removed on these diagnostics. Unknown order excludes a record only from the order sensitivity. Baseline MAIA can moderate the condition contrast; its single pre-experiment measurement cannot mediate an intervention-induced change in MAIA. No mediation analysis was fitted, and order adjustment does not establish absence of carryover.

== Statistical analysis
<study5:sec:stats>
The analysis plan was registered on AsPredicted (#link("https://aspredicted.org/az9f89.pdf");). The browser implementation follows the registered questions as operationalised in this Methods section, with a fixed executable contract in `workbook/ANALYSIS_PLAN.md`. It reads the released tables containing 42 participant records and treats participant ID as the repeated-measures unit. An audit subsequently identified two linked behavioural recordings with different questionnaire responses; full-release independence is therefore provisional pending source mapping. Audio--Tactile and Baseline hits with recorded RT are pooled within person to calculate the mean and sample SD; values within mean $plus.minus$ 2.5 SD, inclusive, enter RT analyses. Participants with Catch false-alarm rate greater than 30% are excluded; low hit-rate flags are descriptive. A 200-ms lower cutoff is a sensitivity, not an additional primary rule. The partial session is retained primarily, with explicit exclusion sensitivities. Interactive preparation controls do not change this analysis population.

The primary question uses a Gaussian ML mixed model of individual Audio--Tactile RTs, with Condition, instructed Phase, numeric SOA, all interactions, and a participant random intercept. Control/Viscereality and Exhale/Inhale are coded $- .5 \/ + .5$; SOA is centred and divided by its trial-level sample SD. A likelihood-ratio test compares the full model to the identical model without the three-way term. The secondary model tests Phase $times$ SOA within Control. Five-level SOA coding is a sensitivity because coding was not fully specified in the registration. Random-intercept ML is profiled exactly using participant sufficient statistics and Woodbury identities, including the zero-variance boundary; it is independently checked against native `statsmodels` ML fits. Model-based normal Wald intervals condition on estimated covariance parameters and are not small-sample-corrected intervals.

Sensitivities include a participant random SOA slope; linear and log-RT regressions with participant-cluster-robust sandwich covariance; equal-participant contrasts of slopes through five SOA means; complete-person $2 times 2 times 5$ repeated-measures ANOVA with effect-specific Greenhouse--Geisser correction; condition-order subgroup descriptions; participant-cluster-robust binomial hit-probability regression; and RT regression with within-condition trial position and Condition $times$ Phase-specific time trends. The random-slope covariance is estimated by Cholesky parameterisation with L-BFGS-B and Powell candidates and a zero-slope boundary. Cluster covariance uses the $G \/ \( G - 1 \) thin \( n - 1 \) \/ \( n - p \)$ correction and normal Wald inference. The participant-slope sign-flip test uses all sign patterns for at most 16 people and otherwise 19,999 Monte Carlo draws with seed 20261015. These analyses probe assumptions rather than replace the primary estimand.

The post hoc missing-RT stress tests assign each hit-labelled Audio--Tactile trial lacking an RT its observed participant $times$ Condition $times$ Phase $times$ SOA cell median. Signed shifts of 0, $plus.minus 100$, and $plus.minus 200$~ms are then applied according to the three-way-contrast direction, and the RT trim and primary model are rerun. These scenarios do not impute misses or identify the missingness mechanism.

The boundary analysis requires trial-level sigmoid $R^2 gt.eq .70$ in all four participant Condition $times$ Phase cells. The registration did not fully specify the four-parameter form, bounds, trial-versus-mean $R^2$, or whether this exclusion also applied to the raw-RT interaction. We explicitly apply it to boundary inference and report the raw-RT interaction for the roster-based cohort. The transparent reimplementation is $f \( x \) = a + \( b - a \) "expit" \( \( x - m \) \/ s \)$, with $a , b in \[ - 2000 , 2000 \]$~ms, midpoint $m$ within the sampled distance range, and width $s in \[ .1 , 200 \]$~cm, using three starts. These are disclosed computational choices, not recovered historical optimizer settings. Registered approximate distances and trajectory-implied positions are fitted separately, after subtracting the participant Condition/Phase mean Baseline. Trial objectives use exact SOA sufficient statistics. We additionally calculate the saturated five-SOA trial-level $R^2$ ceiling, which bounds any deterministic SOA curve independently of sigmoid bounds. In-sample sigmoid/linear RMSE, five-mean fits, group fits, and parameter-bound hits are descriptive. Four-parameter fits to five means have one residual degree of freedom and cannot rescue failed trial gates. Boundary midpoint/sharpness and related MAIA-2 or pictographic associations require four qualifying, bound-free trial fits per person; otherwise these outcomes are explicitly not estimable.

A separate post hoc extension examined whether one common procedure could describe four group RT profiles with interpretable sigmoid transitions. Audiotactile and related visuotactile work motivates examining distance-dependent transitions @canzoneri2012dynamic@noel2020rapid, while expectancy-controlled logarithmic patterns and critical experimental evidence make the response form an empirical question @hobeika2019capturing@holmes2020sounds. This extension addresses group-profile description; it cannot recover participant boundaries that fail the inference gate. Candidate families were developed after inspecting the data and were not registered. Reporting the full finite grid follows the transparency rationale of multiverse analysis @Steegen2016, without claiming an exhaustive set of reasonable analyses or treating the most sigmoid-looking result as a confirmatory finding.

Kernel local-constant and local-linear regression provide established signal-smoothing estimators @LiRacine2007, while local weighted regression, curvature regularization, and isotonic projection provide alternative smooth or shape-constrained approximations @Cleveland1979@Wahba1990@Busing2022. The four-parameter logistic supplies descriptive location and scale parameters @Ritz2019; whether the measurements identify them is a separate question from whether the curve fits well @Raue2009. These sources motivate the estimation tools, not particular bandwidths, bounds, or a requirement that PPS observations follow a sigmoid. The companion's miniature literature review maps each choice to supporting and contrary evidence and records the limits of source access.

The extension used the same retained Audio--Tactile trials and five design positions. Within each participant $times$ Condition $times$ Phase $times$ position cell, five extraction methods were considered: arithmetic mean, median, 10% and 20% per-tail trimmed means, and 20% per-tail winsorized means. Winsorization replaces the lowest and highest $floor.l .2 n floor.r$ values by the adjacent retained order statistics; it does not remove further trials. The resulting cells were averaged with equal participant weights. Robust summaries probe the influence of extreme RTs and cutoff choices @Ratcliff1993; the specific percentages remain exploratory. Mean, median and winsorized summaries target different summaries of the response distribution, and the midpoint of a curve through group summaries is not generally the mean of individual midpoints. Thirty-two smoothing specifications crossed Gaussian local-constant and local-linear bandwidths, tricube local-linear bandwidths, discrete curvature penalties, isotonic regression, and Gaussian smoothing followed by isotonic projection, including an unsmoothed comparator. Separate versus common widths across the four curves gave $5 times 32 times 2 = 320$ pipelines. Tricube smoothing here is a fixed-bandwidth local-linear implementation without robust residual reweighting; the discrete penalty is not an exact cubic smoothing spline. The complete grid is executable in `descriptive_sigmoid.py`.

Distances were normalized to $\[ 0 , 1 \]$ using the registered 10--100~cm range. Each candidate fitted $a + A "expit" \( \( d - m \) \/ s \)$ with $A in \[ 0 , 500 \]$~ms, $m in \[ 10 , 100 \]$~cm and $s in \[ 4.5 , 90 \]$~cm. Increasing RT with distance was imposed as a descriptive shape constraint, allowing $A = 0$ rather than forcing a nonzero transition. Offset and amplitude were profiled analytically over 61 equally spaced midpoints and 35 geometrically spaced widths. The selected method used a finer 241-by-81 grid. The width floor prevents transitions substantially narrower than the smallest sampled spacing: its 10--90% span is 19.8~cm, versus a minimum 17-cm gap. These bounds are explicit regularization choices and were expanded in a sensitivity analysis. No display interpolation was treated as an additional observation.

The same fixed six participant folds (seed 20261016) and five omitted positions evaluated every candidate. For each participant fold and position, extraction, group averaging, smoothing and fitting used only the other participants at the other four positions. The predicted held-out value was centered on the training curve's four unsmoothed extracted values; the fixed scoring target was each test participant's raw cell mean at the omitted position minus that participant's raw mean across the other four positions. Squared errors were averaged over participants, groups and held-out positions. This common shape target permits comparison of mean and robust extraction despite their different overall RT levels. The pipeline with minimum RMSE was selected uniformly for all four curves. Constant and linear benchmarks used identical folds and targets. These reused tuning scores do not constitute an independent post-selection test. Evaluation conditions on profiles cleaned beforehand: participant-wide trimming thresholds use all eligible AT and Baseline hit RTs, including the position later omitted from fitting. Thresholds are not shared across people, but this is not end-to-end prospective prediction with preprocessing learned without the omitted position. The target measures relative profile shape, not absolute RT accuracy.

